answer_text:
Let \(N=25!\). Since \(p\) and \(q\) are relatively prime integers and \(pq=N\), no prime factor of \(N\) can be split between \(p\) and \(q\). Thus each full prime power in the factorization of \(25!\) must go entirely to \(p\) or entirely to \(q\).

Factor \(25!\):
\[
25!=2^{22}3^{10}5^6 7^3 11^2\cdot 13\cdot 17\cdot 19\cdot 23.
\]
So \(p\) must be a product of some of these nine prime-power factors, and \(q\) is the product of the remaining ones. The condition \(p<q\) selects one choice from each complementary pair. Since \(25!\) is not a square, there is no tie \(p=q\). Hence there are
\[
2^{9-1}=256
\]
such fractions.

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\left\{
\frac{\prod_{r\in S} r^{e_r}}{\prod_{r\notin S} r^{e_r}}
:\;
S\subseteq\{2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\},
\;
\prod_{r\in S}r^{e_r}<\prod_{r\notin S}r^{e_r}
\right\},\\
&\text{where }
(e_2,e_3,e_5,e_7,e_{11},e_{13},e_{17},e_{19},e_{23})
=(22,10,6,3,2,1,1,1,1).
\end{aligned}
}
\]

Equivalently, distribute the nine factors
\[
2^{22},\;3^{10},\;5^6,\;7^3,\;11^2,\;13,\;17,\;19,\;23
\]
between the numerator and denominator without splitting any factor, and keep only the resulting fractions whose numerator is smaller than their denominator. There are \(\boxed{256}\) such fractions in total.
